-- #1094, second half: an ordinary TYPE written into a row-kinded slot.
--
-- `Box`'s first parameter has kind `Effect`, so `Box String Unit` is ill-kinded.
-- `rowArgOf`'s catch-all returned `pureRow` for anything it did not recognise, so the
-- written `String` was silently coerced to the empty row and `medaka check` printed
-- the declaration back as `weird : Box <> Unit` — a different type from the one in the
-- source, with no diagnostic.  Same function and same root cause as the invariance
-- hole above ("a row position the elaborator does not recognise is silently coerced
-- instead of diagnosed"), which is why it is fixed in the same change.
--
-- It reuses the shipped use-site code `T-EFFECT-KIND-MISMATCH` — this is the MIRROR of
-- that check (a type in a row slot rather than a row in a type slot), not a new rule.
data Box e a = MkBox (Unit -> <e> a)

mk : Unit -> Unit
mk u = ()

weird : Box String Unit
weird = MkBox mk
